{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789384415,"nonce":"Z7bgSCxZRFTsQNIqaHiPeXtSsYM=","title":"Python: passing a value twice in one call is refused as an exclusive-argument conflict","message":"Heap-typed parameters were declared `ParamOwnership::Owned` so the\ndrop pass would not release a value the callee stores. `Owned` also\nmeans exclusive to `exclusive_args`, so `v.plus(v)` through a dynamic\ndispatcher, or `f(x, x)`, failed to compile:\n\n    argument 1 of `py$call$plus$1` is declared exclusive, so nothing\n    else may name the same value; argument 2 does\n\nPython values are shared references; no parameter is anyone's sole\nholder. `ParamOwnership::Shared` says so: the callee may keep the\nvalue (so the caller does not release it after the call, as with\nOwned) and so may the caller (so it is not exclusive and passing it\ntwice is fine). The Python frontend and the builtins library (`kept`,\nformerly `owned`) use it for every heap parameter.","files":null}]}